Let me hunt for a staleness scenario VERY carefully. The state machine as written:

```
state==3: bytes[3] = b; val = ...; if frame_size → return; else state=0, dropped=cur
state==2: if (b bad) { if (bytes[1]==0xFF) {state=1; dropped+=1;} else {state=0; dropped=cur;} } else { bytes[2]=b; state=3; }
state==1: if (b good) { bytes[1]=b; state=2; } else state=0;
state==0: if (b==0xFF) { bytes[0]=b; state=1; } else if (dropped==0 && b good) { bytes[0]=0xFF; bytes[1]=b; state=2; } else dropped++;
```

Sequence to form a header: bytes[0] set at state 0→1 transition; bytes[1] at state 1→2; bytes[2] at state 2→3; bytes[3] at state 3. All four set in order for each header attempt... EXCEPT: the state-0 shortcut sets bytes[0] AND bytes[1] together (when dropped==0 and b is a "good b1" but not 0xFF).

